# Stack

## At a glance

| Component | Choice | Decided |
| --- | --- | --- |
| frontend | Next.js | MUSTARD |
| database | PostgreSQL | MUSTARD |
| storage | Cloudflare R2 | MUSTARD |
| auth | Auth0 | You (locked) |

## frontend: Next.js

Next.js gives you a single well-trodden framework for the screens and the server bits, which is exactly what a habit tracker on the web needs.

**Alternatives considered**

- **Remix** — Great data handling, smaller ecosystem.
- **SvelteKit** — Lighter to write, fewer hired hands know it.

## database: PostgreSQL

A rock-solid relational database that fits the Habit and CheckIn tables cleanly and scales well past your first hundreds of users.

**Alternatives considered**

- **SQLite** — Zero setup, awkward once you have many writers.
- **MySQL** — Also solid, slightly weaker JSON support.

## storage: Cloudflare R2

Because people upload files, you need somewhere durable to keep them; S3 is the default the whole ecosystem is built around.

**Alternatives considered**

- **Cloudflare R2** — No egress fees, newer tooling.
- **Supabase Storage** — Bundled with the DB, less control.

## auth: Auth0

_You already decided this — it is locked and will survive any redo._

A hosted sign-in service so you do not hand-roll password security; drops into a Next.js app in an afternoon.

**Alternatives considered**

- **Auth.js** — Free and flexible, more wiring to do.
- **Supabase Auth** — Bundled with the DB, less polished UI.
"
